{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And Joseph saw his brothers and he recognized them</b> — As soon as he saw them he recognized them and feared lest they recognize him, \"and he made himself strange to them\" — for he placed the mitznefet on his forehead and part of his face and altered his appearance, as it is said of Jeroboam's wife (I Kings 14:2), \"Arise now and disguise yourself, and they will not know that you are Jeroboam's wife,\" and it is written (I Kings 14:5), \"And it came to pass when she came, that she disguised herself.\" Or \"and he made himself strange\" here may mean that he made himself a stranger to them through his words, for he spoke harshly to them and said to them in anger, as though no man comes before him to buy grain — \"From where do you come?\" and they said, \"From the land of Canaan to buy food\"; and when they mentioned this to him, then it became clear to him that they were truly his brothers. And this is the sense of \"And Joseph recognized\" a second time, for there was added to him regarding them recognition and certain knowledge. And Rashi wrote (Rashi on Genesis 42:7) that he made himself like a stranger to them in speech, to speak harshly with them. According to his opinion, \"and he made himself strange\" means that he spoke like a stranger. But this is not correct:"], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 42, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:43:49.924417107Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}